{"name":"Fading Horizon","hex":"#442266","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fading","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of fade."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The process by which something fades; gradual diminishment.","An Irish dance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fading"},{"term":"horizon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible horizontal line (in all directions) where the sky appears to meet the earth in the distance.","The range or limit of one's knowledge, experience or interest; a boundary or threshold."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/horizon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fading","blocks":[{"text":"In wireless communications, fading is the variation of signal attenuation over variables like time, geographical position, and radio frequency. Fading is often modeled as a random process. In wireless systems, fading may either be due to multipath propagation, referred to as multipath-induced fading, weather, or shadowing from obstacles affecting the wave propagation, sometimes referred to as shadow fading."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/330px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fading"},{"title":"Horizon","blocks":[{"text":"Most commonly, the horizon is the border between the surface of a celestial body and its sky when viewed from the perspective of an observer on or above the surface of the celestial body. This concept is further refined as:"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Horizons.svg/330px-Horizons.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizon"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/500px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/500px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frequency-selective time-varying fading causes a cloudy pattern to appear on a spectrogram. Time is shown on the horizontal axis, frequency on the vertical axis and signal strength as grey-scale intensity.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARadio_propagation_noise.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Coherence_time.png/500px-Coherence_time.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Coherence_time.png/500px-Coherence_time.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoherence_time.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/500px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/1280px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/500px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoherence_bandwidth.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/500px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_vi